etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4176903\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: thedailystar.net\nSLUG: north-korea-says-xis-visit-produced-far-reaching-blueprint-ties-4195096\nTEXT: China\'s state-run Xinhua news agency reported that Xi Jinping reached an "important consensus" with North Korean leader Kim on developing China-DPRK relations in the new era. Xi pushed to strengthen diplomatic, law enforcement and military ties with North Korea, aiming to "directly assess technological changes within the North Korean military" and "collect intelligence for monitoring trends in pro-Russian and pro-Chinese human networks". Despite Xi\'s trip following talks with Trump on denuclearization, official media reports made no mention of the goal. Analysts suggest that Beijing\'s tacit acceptance of North Korea\'s nuclear status is evident, as Kim has repeatedly vowed not to give up his nuclear arsenal. China remains an economic anchor for North Korea, with $2.6 billion of foreign trade in 2024, nearly 98% of the total.\n\nID: 4175883\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: tempo.co\nSLUG: china-north-korea-boast-better-ties-after-xi-kim-talks\nTEXT: China and North Korea have strengthened their ties, with Beijing increasingly viewing Pyongyang as a strategic partner in addressing international challenges. The two countries agreed to expand cooperation across multiple sectors, including diplomacy and strategic coordination, during a meeting between Chinese Premier Li Keqiang and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un. This marks a shift from previous summits, which focused on repairing and strengthening bilateral ties. China\'s official position advocates for a denuclearized Korean Peninsula, but the Rodong Sinmun and Chinese state media readouts of the summit made no reference to North Korea\'s nuclear program or denuclearization. Instead, the emphasis was on political, military and economic cooperation.\n\nID: 4177146\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2362273\nTEXT: North Korea says Xi\'s visit produced a far-reaching blueprint for ties, with the two countries deepening their revolutionary friendship and close comradely relationship. During his two-day visit, Xi toured the Central Cadres Training School of the Workers\' Party with Kim, discussing party official training and planting a commemorative tree. The two leaders also visited the Friendship Tower memorial honouring Chinese soldiers who fought in the Korean War. Following his trip, Xi sent Kim a letter of gratitude, stating that they made an in-depth exchange of views on issues of mutual interest and achieved important common understanding.\n\nID: 4177230\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: asiaone.com\nSLUG: chinas-xi-says-he-reached-important-consensus-kim-north-korea-visit-kcna-reports\nTEXT: Chinese President Xi Jinping reached an "important consensus" with North Korean leader Kim Jong-un during his visit to Pyongyang, North Korea. Xi and Kim exchanged in-depth views on common issues and agreed to safeguard regional and global peace, with Xi expressing willingness to work with Kim based on fundamental interests. The visit was successfully concluded, marking the beginning of a new historical stage in bilateral ties.\n\n\n',)
