etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4177536\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: thestar.com\nSLUG: article-43e33609-048e-5151-a1b6-a289e355a0e9-html\nTEXT: Asian shares fell after a tech sell-off on Wall Street, with Japan and South Korea leading the decline. Oil prices gained after surging following US military attacks on Iran, but later pared their gains. The US military launched attacks against Iran after a helicopter crash near the Strait of Hormuz, which President Donald Trump blamed on Tehran. The situation remains highly volatile, with demand for oil strong in the early summer adding to upward pressure on prices. US futures edged lower, following losses for chipmakers including Micron Technology, Advanced Micro Devices (AMD), and Marvell Technology. South Korea\'s Kospi dropped 4.5%, while Tokyo\'s Nikkei 225 fell 1.9%. Shares of Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix plummeted, while Tokyo Electron advanced.\n\nID: 4176068\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: thestar.com\nSLUG: article-b4176421-2241-5683-a47e-c3d4c2dc265d-html\nTEXT: Asian shares are mixed as tech stocks rebound from sell-offs, while oil prices slip. The 10-year Treasury yield fell to 4.52% from 4.56%, still above its pre-Iran war level of 3.97%. The US inflation updates, including consumer prices and wholesale prices, will arrive later this week. Traders expect the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates at least once by year-end, which could slow the economy and undercut stock prices. The average long-term US mortgage rate hit a nine-month high, discouraging AI data center construction. Airline stocks rose after oil prices dropped, with American Airlines up 3.6% and Delta Air Lines gaining 3.8%.\n\nID: 4176866\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: asian-stocks-oil-middle-east-tensions-6172706\nTEXT: Asian stocks plummeted on Wednesday, June 10, as oil prices surged due to escalating tensions in the Middle East. The US launched strikes against Iran after President Donald Trump claimed Tehran had shot down a US Apache helicopter in the Strait of Hormuz, leaving investors on edge. The MSCI\'s broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan dropped 0.6%, while Japan\'s Nikkei fell 0.9% and South Korea\'s KOSPI slumped 2%. Oil prices rose 1% in early trade, moving away from a seven-week low.\n\nID: 4176652\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: mainichi.jp\nSLUG: 022000c\nTEXT: Tokyo stocks plummeted as fresh US strikes on Iran escalated Mideast tensions. The Tokyo Stock Exchange dropped 1.3% in morning trading, with key indices such as the Nikkei 225 and the Tokyo Stock Exchange 50 Index also falling. The US strikes on Iran\'s military sites, which occurred in the early hours of June 10, heightened concerns about a potential conflict in the Middle East.\n\nID: 4176653\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: mainichi.jp\nSLUG: 034000c\nTEXT: Tokyo stocks dropped due to heightened Mideast tensions, with caution ahead of the US CPI release.\n\n\n',)
