etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4176338\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: scrippsnews.com\nSLUG: southern-baptist-convention-weighs-stricter-ban-on-churches-with-women-pastors\nTEXT: The Southern Baptist Convention is voting on a constitutional amendment to ban churches with women pastors, a proposal that has been met with opposition from advocates of women in pastoral roles. The amendment, proposed by Albert Mohler, president of Southern Baptist Theological Seminary in Kentucky, would require a two-thirds vote in two consecutive years. This is the fourth year that messengers are voting on an amendment regarding women pastors, with previous years failing to attain the necessary two-thirds majority. The Baptist Faith and Message, the denomination\'s statement of belief, opposes women pastors, but its churches are independent. The proposed amendment specifically bans churches where women have the office of pastor or function as one, including preaching to the assembled congregation.\n\nID: 4176188\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: wsvn.com\nSLUG: southern-baptist-convention-weighs-stricter-ban-on-churches-with-women-pastors\nTEXT: The Southern Baptist Convention is weighing a stricter ban on churches with women pastors, with the proposed constitutional amendment requiring a two-thirds vote in two consecutive years. The current version of the amendment, proposed by Albert Mohler, president of Southern Baptist Theological Seminary in Kentucky, would specifically ban churches where women have the office of pastor or are functioning as one. This is the fourth year in a row that church representatives will vote on an amendment regarding women pastors, with previous attempts failing to attain the required two-thirds majority.\n\nID: 4179759\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: ocregister.com\nSLUG: southern-baptist-convention-votes-ban-women-pastors\nTEXT: The Southern Baptist Convention (SBC) has voted to ban women pastors, with some churches already expelled for having female senior pastoral roles. This decision stands in contrast to other liberal Protestant denominations, such as the Catholic and Orthodox churches, which ordain only men to the priesthood. The Baptist Women in Ministry organization has expressed solidarity with women pastors who have been harmed by this vote, lamenting the lack of affirmation and respect for their ministry.\n\n\n',)
