etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4179605\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: apnews.com\nSLUG: stanley-cup-final-hurricanes-067cfa68c68388760a5057260484ce65\nTEXT: The Carolina Hurricanes are one step closer to the Stanley Cup Finals after a thrilling 5-4 overtime win over the Vegas Golden Knights in Game 2. The Hurricanes trailed 2-0 in the first period, but rallied to win on Seth Jarvis\' one-timer in overtime. The series is now tied 1-1, with the Golden Knights leading 9-1 in the second period. Despite this, the Hurricanes have had the edge in the third period, outscoring Vegas 10-3. Defenseman Jaccob Slavin attributed the team\'s success to sticking to their game plan, saying "it\'s just whoever can play their game better and more consistently."\n\nID: 4176736\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: nypost.com\nSLUG: hurricanes-even-stanley-cup-final-after-jordan-staals-two-goal-night-in-game-4-win\nTEXT: The Carolina Hurricanes evened the Stanley Cup Final with a 5-3 win over the Vegas Golden Knights on June 9, 2026. Jordan Staal scored his second goal of the game while stretched out on his stomach at 6:32 of the third period, putting the Hurricanes ahead for good. The win gives Carolina a chance to potentially have two games on home ice to win its first Cup in 20 years. The Golden Knights are seeking their second title in four years, with Game 5 scheduled for Thursday night at Carolina.\n\n\n',)
