etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4179932\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: ibtimes.sg\nSLUG: who-jeffery-lee-judge-blocks-alabama-nitrogen-gas-execution-major-death-penalty-ruling-87737\nTEXT: Judge Jeffrey Lee Judge blocked Alabama\'s nitrogen gas execution protocol, citing it causes intense suffering lasting between one and three minutes. In a federal bench trial on May 28, Judge Marks initially ruled against Lee, finding the protocol did not cause more suffering than necessary. However, the 11th Circuit reversed this decision on June 9, stating that Alabama\'s nitrogen hypoxia protocol triggers "air hunger", an oxygen deprivation response producing extreme physiological need for oxygen, resulting in prolonged suffering.\n\nID: 4177295\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71540027\nTEXT: A federal court has blocked Alabama\'s scheduled execution by nitrogen, citing it as unconstitutional. The decision, made by Judge Marks, blocks the use of this new execution method but does not prevent the state from using its other authorized methods, lethal injection and the electric chair. The ruling notes that no method of execution is immune to constitutional challenge, but it does not guarantee a painless death. The state has two other authorized methods and can switch to Lee\'s preferred method, a firing squad, if he so chooses.\n\n\n',)
