etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4177280\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: tempo.co\nSLUG: afghanistan-taliban-open-fire-during-hijab-protest\nTEXT: The Taliban opened fire during a hijab protest in Herat, Afghanistan, sparking concerns over the group\'s strict interpretation of Islamic law. The Ministry for the Propagation of Virtue and the Prevention of Vice, which enforces the rules, denied rumors of women being arrested for wearing the hijab. However, human rights monitors verified at least 16 detentions in Herat since Friday over alleged non-compliance with dress requirements, including a pregnant woman. Fereshta Abbasi, an Afghanistan researcher for Human Rights Watch, expressed concern over the "arbitrary detention of women for so-called inappropriate clothing" and called on the Taliban to release those detained peacefully. Richard Bennett, the UN\'s investigator on human rights in Afghanistan, was "alarmed by excessive use of force against seemingly peaceful protesters" and urged those responsible to be held accountable.\n\nID: 4176293\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 667938-police-crack-down-on-anti-hijab-protests-in-western-afghanistan\nTEXT: The Taliban regime in Afghanistan has imposed strict dress code requirements, leading to protests in the western city of Herat. According to Sayed Masoud Hosseini, spokesperson for Herat police, the protests in the Jebrail area "created tensions" and disturbed public order. Witnesses reported that officials from the Ministry for the Promotion of Virtue and Prevention of Vice attempted to arrest women opposing the mandatory dress requirements, targeting those who were already observing the required code. The protests featured fully veiled women among the demonstrators, with some residents claiming officials targeted those who were already following the dress code. The UN Assistance Mission in Afghanistan expressed concern over reports of women detained for allegedly failing to meet dress requirements, urging the Taliban authorities to respect freedom of movement and equality before the law.\n\n\n',)
